#v(40pt)

#only("1-")[- Systematic deviations from rationality 📏]
#only("2-")[- Affect financial decisions 💰]
#only("3-")[- Examples: overconfidence, confirmation bias, loss aversion, anchoring, herd behavior, recency bias, sunk cost fallacy, hindsight bias, availability heuristic, representativeness heuristic 📜]

#only("1")[
#voiceover("Cognitive biases are systematic patterns of deviation from norm or rationality in judgment.")
]

#only("2")[
#voiceover("These biases can significantly affect financial decisions, often leading to suboptimal outcomes.")
]

#only("3")[
#voiceover("Some common examples of cognitive biases include overconfidence bias, which leads to excessive risk-taking; confirmation bias, which causes people to seek information that confirms their preconceptions; loss aversion, where people prefer avoiding losses over acquiring equivalent gains; anchoring bias, relying too heavily on the first piece of information encountered; herd behavior, following the actions of a larger group; recency bias, weighing recent events more heavily; sunk cost fallacy, continuing an endeavor due to past investments; hindsight bias, seeing past events as predictable; availability heuristic, overestimating likelihood based on memory; and representativeness heuristic, judging probability based on resemblance.")
]

]

